<p dir=3D"auto">Flux is an application design paradigm used as a replacemen=
t for the more traditional MVC pattern. Flux is a Javascript architecture o=
r pattern for UI which runs on a unidirectional data flow and has a central=
ized dispatcher. It is useful when your project has dynamic data and you ne=
ed to keep the data updated in an effective manner. It was created by Faceb=
ook, and complements React as view.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Architecture:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">The Flux architecture is based on the following components:=
</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong>Store/ Stores</strong>: Serves as a container for the app state=
 &amp; logic</li>
<li><strong>Action</strong>: Enables data passing to the dispatcher</li>
<li><strong>View</strong>: Same as the view in MVC architecture, but in the=
 context of React components</li>
<li><strong>Dispatcher</strong>: Coordinates actions &amp; updates to store=
s</li>
</ul>
<p align=3D"center" dir=3D"auto">
  <a target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" href=3D"https://github.c=
om/learning-zone/react-basics/blob/master/assets/flux-architecture.png"><im=
g src=3D"https://github.com/learning-zone/react-basics/raw/master/assets/fl=
ux-architecture.png" alt=3D"Flux Architecture" width=3D"500px" style=3D"max=
-width: 100%;"></a>
</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">In the Flux architecture, when a user clicks on something, =
the view creates actions. Action can create new data and send it to the dis=
patcher. The dispatcher then dispatches the action result to the appropriat=
e store. The store updates the state based on the result and sends an updat=
e to the view.</p>
